WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:07.710
Knee arthroscopy is used in
the diagnosis and treatment

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.710
of a number of knee
disorders and injuries.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:14.330
One common use is in the
treatment of meniscal tears.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.680
Under normal
conditions, the menisci,

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.760
C-shaped pieces of cartilage
resting between the femur

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.670
and tibia, provide
cushioning and stability

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:23.920
for your knee joint.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.800
Injuries, or arthritis,
can cause the menisci

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.460
to become torn, resulting
in knee pain, swelling,

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.290
and diminished range of motion.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.420
Knee arthroscopy
is often performed

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.450
to remove the damaged
portion of the meniscus.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.080
When you arrive at the
hospital for your procedure,

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.130
an intravenous line
will be started,

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.870
and you may be given a
sedative to help you relax.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:48.390
The procedure can be performed
under spinal anesthesia, which

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.580
keeps you numb from
the waist down;

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.850
local anesthesia, which
keeps your knee area numb;

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.060
or less commonly,
general anesthesia, which

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.180
renders you unconscious for
the duration of the procedure.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.620
An arthroscopic
meniscectomy usually

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:05.670
takes between 45 and 90 minutes.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.170
Your surgeon will gain
access to your knee joint

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.010
by using sharp instruments,
called trocars,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:15.370
to make two or three
small openings, or ports.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.820
He or she will inject
a sterile solution

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.650
into the joint to push
the surfaces apart

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:22.750
allowing the structures
inside the joint

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.610
to be viewed more easily.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.640
Next, your surgeon will
insert the arthroscope

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.900
and other instruments
through the various ports.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.070
Images from the
arthroscope's camera

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.250
are magnified and projected
onto a video monitor.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.190
Your surgeon will carefully
examine the inside of your knee

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:43.490
joint, locate the damage, remove
any loose or severely injured

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.840
parts, and use a
shaving instrument

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.970
to smooth any jagged edges.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.370
While it may be possible
to repair the meniscus

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.100
with small stitches,
in most cases,

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.690
the damaged portion
must be removed.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.790
At the end of the
procedure, your surgeon

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.440
will, again, carefully
examine the entire knee

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.160
for any areas of
bleeding or other damage,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.780
remove the arthroscope
and other instruments,

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.810
and close each of the keyhole
incisions with a few sutures.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.960
After your surgery, you'll
be taken to the recovery area

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.140
for monitoring where
you will be given pain

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:16.970
medication as needed.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.870
Most patients leave the
outpatient surgery unit

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.990
within a few hours.

